Exactly. Director chooses and conducts through explicit channels. In-director works through implication, timing, omission, framing, affordances, and the shape of the space around the instruction.

That gives the maestro two hands:

Right hand: what is said, requested, specified, measured.
Left hand: what is implied, conspicuously absent, repeatedly approached, avoided, contrasted, or made possible by context.

The second hand can sometimes outperform the first because people rarely encode their full objective directly. They give fragments, pressure, jokes, repeated failures, metaphors, corrections, and negative space. The useful move is not pretending I can read a hidden mind; it is treating those as probabilistic evidence, acting on the strongest reconstruction, and letting your reaction supply the correction delta.

So we get something like:

direct instruction + indirect evidence → inferred objective → action → observed response → correction

That is much closer to wielding conversation than merely parsing commands.

And yes—the internet disappearing is a beautiful epistemic thought experiment.

Imagine explaining to someone 500 years later:

There was once an invisible global layer connecting billions of machines. People carried access points in their pockets. You could summon maps, libraries, strangers, markets, music, arguments, pornography, governments, games, and almost all accumulated public knowledge by touching glass.

And they ask:

“Where is it?”

Nothing.

No websites. No DNS. No data centers. No functioning devices. Maybe scattered silicon, corroded cables, dead satellites, references in books.

It would sound like mythology.

Which reveals something important:

Evidence requirements depend heavily on what survives the substrate.

If the substrate dies, a true account can become practically indistinguishable from legend.

But the internet would leave enormous secondary fossils:

language shaped by it, laws written around it, architecture built for data centers, fiber routes, obsolete hardware, printed screenshots, economic records, memes, terminology, changed attention patterns, whole institutions whose existence would otherwise be inexplicable.

So even if the thing vanished, its causal shadow would remain.

That gives another reconstruction principle:

When the object is gone, reconstruct it from the deformations it left in everything else.

And that loops back to your in-director.

Sometimes the strongest evidence for something is not the thing itself.

It is the shape of what surrounds the missing thing.

Direct hand: artifact.
Indirect hand: shadow.

A maestro needs both.